  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 223 AM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nCold front has pushed across northeast Colorado in these wee\nmorning hours. This will support cooler weather today, although \nhigh temperatures will still be near normal for this time of year.\nA couple showers may linger over the northern plains early this \nmorning, with redevelopment mainly over the mountains and Palmer \nDivide area this afternoon where airmass is less strongly capped. \nA few of these high based showers and storms could drift onto the \nadjacent plains as usual late in the afternoon/evening, but no \nsignificant precipitation is expected. \n\nWe'll see low level moisture gradually return to the eastern\nplains tonight, with areas of stratus likely developing \nalong/east of a Limon to Sterling line. That does offer up some \nuncertainty for Sunday's high temperatures. Lee troughing indeed \nlooks slow to develop, and thus the cooler and more moist boundary\nlayer conditions will likely persist more than most models \nadvertise. While Denver and the I-25 Corridor is still shaping up\nto be quite hot (lower 90s), somewhat cooler weather will likely \nhold over the eastern plains. While we've trimmed those \ntemperatures from previous forecasts, further trimming is quite \nlikely for the eastern plains due to the morning stratus and \ndeeper moist boundary layer. Regarding convection, the mountains \nwill see an uptick in convective coverage and intensity as another\nmonsoonal moisture plume pushes northeast into the state. \nPrecipitable Water values increase to >150% of normal over the \nmountains, and thus a couple stronger storms could produce brief \nheavy rain. They will likely tend to weaken as they attempt to \nmove off the higher terrain given locally drier airmass. At this \ntime, the prospects of these tapping into the richer air over the\neastern plains also seems slim. \n\nFor Monday, it still looks quite warm with more pronounced lee\ntroughing and downslope in west-southwest flow aloft. Regarding\nconvective potential, the plume of mid/upper level moisture shifts\nacross Colorado, but may end up being pushed too far south/east  \nfor higher shower/storm coverage. PoPs originating from model \nblends were still rather aggressive but wouldn't be surprised to \nsee the higher coverage shift well southeast of the I-76 Corridor,\nespecially considering the eastward scouring of low level \nmoisture ahead of the lee trough. \n\nTuesday through Thursday is shaping up to have the highest\ncoverage of showers/storms for the week ahead. On Tuesday, the\nmonsoonal moisture plume is advertised to shift northward slightly\nas an upper level trough digs into the western U.S. At the same\ntime, a more significant cold front will be pushing southward\nacross the plains, lending itself to a stronger low level upslope\ncomponent east of the Front Range. High temperatures are still\nlooking to cool into the 70s, with a 30-40% chance of highs \nholding below 70F on the plains. For Wednesday and Thursday, the \nforecast area would get into a more favorable location for weak \nsynoptic scale lift as models are in better agreement with a weak \nshortwave lifting slowly east across the northern Great Basin into\nWyoming. We'll still need a weak front or two to help our upslope\nin order to see higher shower/storm coverage, but trends have \nbeen in our favor here for light to locally moderate precipitation\namounts. Trends would then be for drier weather and near normal \ntemperatures for later next week.",
